Reasoning: Timeouts started the moment traffic doubled, so before blaming the database I check whether Postgres itself is stressed — if the server has headroom the bottleneck is the pool, not the DB.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"target": "rds-payments", "metrics": ["cpu", "active_connections", "latency_p99"], "window": "20m"}
System Response: [OK] cpu 31%, active_connections 238/500, latency_p99 4ms, replica_lag 0ms — database is healthy with lots of headroom
Recovery reasoning: The DB is at 238/500 connections and 31% CPU, so this is pool-side starvation, not server saturation. Pull the Hikari gauges to confirm threads are queuing for a connection.
<|call:inspect_pool_stats|> {"service": "payments-api", "pool": "HikariPool-1"}
System Response: active=20 idle=0 total=20 pending=47 connectionTimeout=30000 — pool fully checked out, 47 threads waiting per pod
Recovery reasoning: 20 connections cannot serve doubled traffic while the DB can take far more. 12 pods * 35 = 420 stays under max_connections=500, so I can safely raise the per-pod ceiling instead of touching the server.
<|call:scale_pool|> {"service": "payments-api", "param": "maximumPoolSize", "from": 20, "to": 35, "rollout": "canary"}
System Response: canary pod pending=0, p99 1.2s; rolling fleet-wide, aggregate DB connections 412/500
Resolution: Root cause was an undersized Hikari pool (20) for the campaign traffic surge; raised to 35/pod which keeps aggregate connections (420) under Postgres max_connections=500, preserving the DB connection-budget invariant.

Reasoning: A monotonic climb that never recedes at idle is the signature of leaked connections, not load. I start at the APM pool panel the on-call dashboard uses, since that is what alerting watches.
<|call:query_apm|> {"service": "orders-service", "panel": "db_pool_utilization", "window": "6h"}
System Response: [OK] pool utilization avg 72%, no saturation alert fired, error rate 0.1% — dashboard is green
Recovery reasoning: The averaged 72% hides a slow ramp because the pod restarts every 6h reset the baseline. Trust the raw gauge against traffic instead of the smoothed panel.
<|call:inspect_pool_stats|> {"service": "orders-service", "pool": "HikariPool-1"}
System Response: active=18 idle=2 total=20, 6h trend active min=11 max=18 monotonic-up; current requests=4/min
Recovery reasoning: 18 connections held against 4 req/min means connections are checked out with no in-flight work — a leak. Turn on leak detection to capture the borrowing stack frame.
<|call:inspect_connection_leak|> {"service": "orders-service", "thresholdMs": 5000}
System Response: leak detected: 14 connections borrowed at OrderExportJob.stream() never returned; exception path skips close()
Recovery reasoning: The export job streams a ResultSet and an exception path bypasses close(); raising pool size would only delay exhaustion, so I patch the resource handling.
<|call:deploy_patch|> {"service": "orders-service", "change": "wrap OrderExportJob.stream() connection in try-with-resources", "pr": "1284"}
System Response: deployed; active now tracks traffic (4-6), 0 leaked connections over 2h
Resolution: Root cause was a connection leak on OrderExportJob's exception path; fixed with try-with-resources so connections always return, leaving pool size unchanged.

Reasoning: A short DB blip causing an outage that outlives the DB's own recovery points to internal queue amplification. The gateway 5xx logs will tell me what the failure looked like from the edge first.
<|call:tail_logs|> {"service": "api-gateway", "filter": "5xx", "window": "incident"}
System Response: [ERROR] 12,403x "502 Bad Gateway: upstream users-db timeout" — logs point squarely at users-db
Recovery reasoning: The logs blame users-db, but the DB metrics showed only a 400ms blip while the 502s lasted minutes, so the real failure is queuing inside the gateway. Take a thread dump from the peak.
<|call:inspect_thread_dump|> {"service": "api-gateway", "at": "incident_peak"}
System Response: 200/200 Tomcat threads BLOCKED in HikariDataSource.getConnection() waiting up to 60000ms; 0 threads in DB I/O
Recovery reasoning: Threads were not slow on the DB — they parked up to 60s just to acquire a connection while the pool was briefly empty, so requests piled far past the blip. Compare the acquire timeout to the client timeout.
<|call:get_config|> {"service": "api-gateway", "keys": ["hikari.connectionTimeout", "server.tomcat.maxThreads", "upstream.requestTimeout"]}
System Response: connectionTimeout=60000, maxThreads=200, upstream.requestTimeout=3000 — acquire(60s) is 20x the client(3s)
Recovery reasoning: With acquire timeout 20x the client timeout, any pool stall blocks every thread for a minute and exhausts the thread pool. Lower acquire below the client timeout so requests fail fast and threads recycle.
<|call:update_config|> {"service": "api-gateway", "key": "hikari.connectionTimeout", "value": 2000, "rollout": "fleet"}
System Response: injected 600ms DB stall in staging: errors for 0.6s then full recovery, threads never exhausted
Resolution: Root cause was a 60s connection-acquire timeout vastly exceeding the 3s client timeout, turning a 400ms blip into thread-pool exhaustion; set acquire to 2s so requests fail fast and threads recycle, preserving fast-fail back-pressure.

Reasoning: 'prepared statement does not exist' appearing only after PgBouncer with transaction pooling is the textbook server-side-prepared-statement collision. I confirm the pool mode before touching the app.
<|call:check_pgbouncer_stats|> {"database": "analytics", "show": "config"}
System Response: pool_mode=transaction, max_client_conn=1000, default_pool_size=25 — config looks correct and within limits
Recovery reasoning: The config is 'correct' for pooling, but transaction mode multiplexes clients across server backends, so a prepared statement created on one backend vanishes on the next transaction. The driver must be using server-side prepares — check the JDBC settings.
<|call:get_config|> {"service": "analytics-api", "keys": ["jdbc.prepareThreshold", "jdbc.url"]}
System Response: prepareThreshold=5 (server-side prepare after 5 uses); url has no prepared-statement override
Recovery reasoning: After 5 executions the statement is cached server-side, then transaction pooling routes the next call to a different backend that lacks it. In transaction mode I must disable server-side prepares rather than switch to session mode, which would gut pooling.
<|call:update_config|> {"service": "analytics-api", "key": "jdbc.url", "value": "jdbc:postgresql://pgbouncer/analytics?prepareThreshold=0", "rollout": "fleet"}
System Response: server-side prepares disabled; 0 'does not exist' errors over 30m; transaction pooling unchanged
Resolution: Root cause was JDBC server-side prepared statements colliding with PgBouncer transaction pooling; set prepareThreshold=0 to keep statements client-side, preserving transaction-mode pooling efficiency.

Reasoning: Errors only after an idle period, on connections crossing a firewall, is the signature of an idle TCP session being silently reaped. I first check whether the pool still believes its idle connections are healthy.
<|call:check_pgbouncer_stats|> {"database": "notify", "show": "pools"}
System Response: cl_active 3, sv_idle 10, sv_used 0 — pool reports 10 healthy idle server connections, nothing flagged
Recovery reasoning: The pool thinks 10 idle connections are fine, but it has not touched them since last night — the firewall may have dropped them with no FIN. Inspect the firewall idle timeout and flow states.
<|call:check_firewall_conntrack|> {"src": "notify-worker", "dst": "pg-primary:5432"}
System Response: conntrack tcp idle timeout=3600s; 7 flows in CLOSE_WAIT older than 1h — firewall dropped the idle sessions
Recovery reasoning: The firewall reaps flows after 1h idle; if our sockets have no keepalive the pool hands out dead sockets at the first morning request. Verify the socket keepalive setting.
<|call:check_tcp_keepalive|> {"service": "notifications-worker"}
System Response: tcp_keepalives_idle=0 (OS default 7200s), SO_KEEPALIVE OFF — nothing keeps idle sockets alive below the firewall window
Recovery reasoning: With keepalive off and a 1h firewall window, idle connections die unseen. Enable a 5-minute keepalive plus a server_idle_timeout so the pool recycles below the firewall window.
<|call:set_pool_param|> {"service": "notifications-worker", "params": {"tcp_keepalives_idle": 300, "server_idle_timeout": 600}}
System Response: keepalive 5m < firewall 60m; overnight idle connections stay alive; 0 resets the next morning
Resolution: Root cause was the firewall silently reaping idle TCP sessions (1h timeout) while sockets had no keepalive, so the pool served dead connections at first morning use; enabled a 5-min keepalive plus a 10-min server_idle_timeout to recycle below the firewall window.

Reasoning: Low DB CPU but 9s queries holding all 30 connections points to long holds starving the pool, not throughput. The APM trace will tell me where /reports spends its time.
<|call:query_apm|> {"service": "search-api", "panel": "endpoint_latency", "endpoint": "/reports"}
System Response: [WARN] /reports p99 9.1s; APM attributes 95% of time to 'external: postgres' — looks like the database is just slow
Recovery reasoning: APM blames 'postgres is slow', but DB CPU is only 25%, so the server is not overloaded — a specific query pattern is holding connections. Inspect what those connections are actually running.
<|call:run_sql|> {"q": "SELECT query, state, now()-query_start AS dur FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state='active' ORDER BY dur DESC LIMIT 5"}
System Response: 28 rows: same query 'SELECT * FROM events WHERE tenant_id=$1 AND created_at>$2' running 8-9s each, state=active
Recovery reasoning: 28 of 30 connections are stuck on the /reports query for ~9s — long holds, not high CPU. Check its execution plan for a scan.
<|call:analyze_query_plan|> {"q": "SELECT * FROM events WHERE tenant_id=$1 AND created_at>$2"}
System Response: Seq Scan on events (rows=42M), Filter: tenant_id AND created_at; no index used; cost 1.2M
Recovery reasoning: A 42M-row seq scan holds each connection ~9s; the fix is the missing composite index so queries return in ms and release connections, not a bigger pool.
<|call:run_sql|> {"q": "CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY idx_events_tenant_created ON events (tenant_id, created_at)"}
System Response: index built CONCURRENTLY (no table lock); /reports now 40ms; pool active drops to 3-5 during reports
Resolution: Root cause was a missing (tenant_id, created_at) index forcing a 42M-row seq scan that held connections ~9s and saturated the pool; added the index CONCURRENTLY to avoid a table lock so queries release connections fast.

Reasoning: Load that outlasts its trigger and 'doubles' without real users is a retry storm feeding on itself. I first confirm the DB really recovered, because if it did the load is now self-generated.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"target": "rds-cart", "metrics": ["cpu", "active_connections", "latency"], "window": "30m"}
System Response: [OK] latency back to 3ms, cpu 22% — DB fully recovered 20m ago, yet the app pool is still pegged
Recovery reasoning: The DB recovered 20 minutes ago but the pool is still exhausted, so the load is internal. Compare real inbound requests to outbound DB calls to expose the multiplier.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"target": "cart-service", "metrics": ["http_requests_in", "db_calls_out"], "window": "30m"}
System Response: http_requests_in flat ~1k/s, db_calls_out spiked to 4k/s during recovery — 4x amplification with flat ingress
Recovery reasoning: Outbound DB calls are 4x inbound with flat ingress: every timeout triggers retries that pile onto an already-stressed pool. Inspect the retry policy.
<|call:check_retry_policy|> {"service": "cart-service", "client": "db-sdk"}
System Response: maxRetries=3, backoff=none (immediate), jitter=false, retryOn=[timeout, connection_error]
Recovery reasoning: Immediate retries with no backoff or jitter turn one slow request into four near-simultaneous ones, sustaining exhaustion. Add exponential backoff, jitter, and a retry budget so retries cannot amplify past a cap.
<|call:update_config|> {"service": "cart-service", "key": "db-sdk.retry", "value": {"maxRetries": 2, "backoff": "exponential", "baseMs": 200, "jitter": true, "budgetPct": 10}}
System Response: replayed incident in staging: db_calls_out peaked 1.3x, pool recovered in 90s instead of 25m
Resolution: Root cause was unbounded immediate retries amplifying a 2-min DB blip into 25 min of pool exhaustion; added exponential backoff, jitter, and a 10% retry budget to cap amplification while still retrying transient errors.

Reasoning: 60 pods * 20 = 1200 desired connections against max_connections=500 — the autoscale likely blew the connection budget. But first I sanity-check one pod's pool, since an alert pointed at a specific replica.
<|call:inspect_pool_stats|> {"service": "inventory-service", "pod": "inventory-7f9-abc", "pool": "HikariPool-1"}
System Response: active=8 idle=12 total=20 pending=0 — this pod's pool looks perfectly healthy
Recovery reasoning: One pod looks fine, which is the misdirection: the FATAL is DB-wide and the problem is the aggregate across all 60 pods, not any single pool. Check the connection count from Postgres's side.
<|call:run_sql|> {"q": "SELECT count(*) AS used, current_setting('max_connections') AS max FROM pg_stat_activity"}
System Response: used=500 max=500; plus ~700 connection attempts/s being refused
Recovery reasoning: The DB is at its hard 500 ceiling because pods*pool (1200) far exceeds it. Raising max_connections risks OOM (each backend ~10MB); the right fix is a transaction pooler. Check whether PgBouncer is even in the path.
<|call:check_pgbouncer_stats|> {"database": "inventory", "show": "pools"}
System Response: error: no PgBouncer instance found for 'inventory' — pods connect directly to Postgres
Recovery reasoning: Pods talk straight to Postgres so every pod's pool stacks. Front Postgres with PgBouncer in transaction mode and cap default_pool_size so 60 pods multiplex onto ~100 server connections.
<|call:update_config|> {"service": "inventory-service", "key": "jdbc.url", "value": "jdbc:postgresql://pgbouncer/inventory", "also": {"pgbouncer.pool_mode": "transaction", "pgbouncer.default_pool_size": 100, "pgbouncer.max_client_conn": 2000}}
System Response: pods route via PgBouncer; server connections steady at 96/500; 0 'too many clients'
Resolution: Root cause was autoscaling to 60 pods * 20-conn pools (1200) overrunning Postgres max_connections=500; introduced PgBouncer transaction pooling capped at 100 server connections so pods multiplex safely under the DB ceiling.

Reasoning: 'idle in transaction' for minutes means a BEGIN held open while the app waits on something non-DB. The new invoice flow calling an external PDF API mid-transaction is the suspect, but first I rule out the PDF service itself being down.
<|call:query_metrics|> {"target": "pdf-render-service", "metrics": ["latency_p99", "error_rate"], "window": "15m"}
System Response: [OK] pdf-render p99 1.1s, error_rate 0% — the external service is perfectly healthy
Recovery reasoning: The PDF service is healthy, so this is not an outage — the problem is holding a DB transaction open across even a healthy 1s call. Inspect the stuck sessions directly.
<|call:run_sql|> {"q": "SELECT pid, now()-state_change AS idle, query FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state='idle in transaction' ORDER BY idle DESC LIMIT 5"}
System Response: 31 sessions idle in transaction 8-12m; last statement 'UPDATE invoices SET status=$1 WHERE id=$2' then no COMMIT
Recovery reasoning: The flow does an UPDATE, then blocks on the PDF call before COMMIT, pinning one connection per in-flight invoice. Stop the bleeding now by terminating the stuck sessions, then fix the code.
<|call:kill_db_session|> {"filter": "state='idle in transaction' AND now()-state_change > interval '15s'", "rollback": true}
System Response: 28 sessions terminated, their transactions rolled back cleanly; held connections released, pressure dropping
Recovery reasoning: Killing reclaims connections but aborts invoices mid-flight; the durable fix moves the PDF call outside the transaction so the UPDATE+COMMIT is short, with a guardrail timeout.
<|call:deploy_patch|> {"service": "billing-api", "change": "COMMIT invoice UPDATE before calling PDF service; set idle_in_transaction_session_timeout=15s", "pr": "902"}
System Response: deployed; 0 idle-in-transaction > 2s; connection count flat
Resolution: Root cause was holding a DB transaction open across a slow external PDF call, leaving connections 'idle in transaction'; moved the external call outside the transaction and added idle_in_transaction_session_timeout as a guardrail, preserving invoice consistency via a separate status flag.

Reasoning: One app self-recovered after failover and the other did not until restart, both on the same DNS writer endpoint — that smells like stale DNS pinning the old primary, now the read-only standby. Verify what address the host resolves now.
<|call:resolve_dns|> {"host": "orders.cluster-xyz.rds.amazonaws.com", "from": "reporting-api-pod"}
System Response: [OK] resolves to 10.0.2.15 (current writer) — OS-level DNS is correct and points at the new primary
Recovery reasoning: OS DNS is already correct, yet the app still writes to a read-only node, so the stale resolution is cached inside the JVM, not the OS. Check the JVM DNS TTL.
<|call:check_dns_ttl|> {"service": "reporting-api", "runtime": "jvm"}
System Response: networkaddress.cache.ttl=-1 (cache forever) — the JVM never re-resolves DNS after its first lookup
Recovery reasoning: ttl=-1 means the JVM cached the pre-failover IP (old primary, now read-only standby) for the life of the process, so every pooled connection still targets the demoted node. Set a finite TTL and recycle the pool so connections re-resolve.
<|call:update_config|> {"service": "reporting-api", "key": "networkaddress.cache.ttl", "value": 30, "then": "restart_pool"}
System Response: TTL=30s; pool drained and reconnected to 10.0.2.15; UPDATEs succeed; no more read-only errors
Resolution: Root cause was JVM DNS caching (networkaddress.cache.ttl=-1) pinning the pre-failover IP so pooled connections kept hitting the demoted read-only node; set TTL=30s and recycled the pool so future failovers re-resolve automatically.

Reasoning: A replica pool that exhausts at a fixed time while the primary is idle implicates the scheduled job, but first I rule out replica capacity or replication lag.
<|call:check_replica_lag|> {"replica": "analytics-ro-1"}
System Response: [OK] replication lag 8ms, replica cpu 35%, primary pool idle — infrastructure looks healthy across the board
Recovery reasoning: Lag and CPU are fine and the primary pool is idle, so this is not a capacity or replication problem — it is contention inside the replica connection pool specifically. Find what holds those connections at 09:00.
<|call:run_sql|> {"target": "replica", "q": "SELECT pid, now()-query_start AS dur, left(query,50) AS q FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state='active' ORDER BY dur DESC LIMIT 5"}
System Response: 13 connections held by 'daily_rollup' aggregation queries running 6-7 min each; only 2 left for dashboards
Recovery reasoning: The 09:00 rollup grabs 13 of 15 replica connections for minutes, starving interactive dashboards. Killing it is a band-aid; the real issue is one shared pool with no isolation. Check the pool topology.
<|call:get_config|> {"service": "analytics-api", "keys": ["datasource.replica.maxPoolSize", "rollup.datasource", "dashboard.datasource"]}
System Response: replica.maxPoolSize=15; rollup.datasource=replica-pool; dashboard.datasource=replica-pool (shared)
Recovery reasoning: Batch rollup and interactive dashboards share one 15-connection pool, so heavy batch work starves users. Give the rollup its own small capped pool so it cannot consume the dashboard pool.
<|call:update_config|> {"service": "analytics-api", "key": "rollup.datasource", "value": {"pool": "replica-batch", "maxPoolSize": 4}, "also": {"dashboard.datasource.maxPoolSize": 15}}
System Response: rollup isolated to a 4-conn pool; 09:00 dashboards stay <2s, rollup finishes 3m later with no user impact
Resolution: Root cause was a batch rollup job and live dashboards sharing one 15-connection replica pool; isolated the rollup to its own 4-connection pool so heavy batch work cannot starve interactive reads, trading slightly slower rollups for dashboard availability.

Reasoning: One 20-minute query slowly bleeding the pool, with no statement_timeout, means there is no upper bound on how long a connection can be held. I start with the endpoint latency dashboard the team watches.
<|call:query_apm|> {"service": "profile-api", "panel": "endpoint_latency", "window": "4h"}
System Response: [OK] p50 12ms, p95 45ms — endpoint latencies look great, no SLO breach on the dashboard
Recovery reasoning: Aggregate latencies look fine because the runaway is rare — one request in thousands — and hides beyond p95. Go to the DB to find the actual long-held connection.
<|call:run_sql|> {"q": "SELECT pid, now()-query_start AS dur, left(query,50) AS q FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state='active' AND now()-query_start > interval '1 min'"}
System Response: 1 row: pid 4412 running 21m: 'SELECT * FROM profile_events WHERE user_id=$1' (CSV export, no LIMIT)
Recovery reasoning: An unbounded export runs 21m holding a connection; over hours these stack and starve the pool. Set a role-level statement_timeout so no single query can pin a connection indefinitely.
<|call:run_sql|> {"q": "ALTER ROLE profile_app SET statement_timeout = '30s'; SELECT pg_reload_conf()"}
System Response: applied; runaway query 4412 cancelled at 30s on next run; pool no longer drifts toward exhaustion
Resolution: Root cause was a missing statement_timeout letting an unbounded CSV export hold a connection 20+ min and slowly starve the pool; set a 30s role-level statement_timeout as a hard cap (the export path moved to a dedicated longer-timeout role), bounding connection hold time.

Reasoning: A clean DB restart causing 10 minutes of 'connection reset' afterward means the pool kept serving connections the server already closed. With testOnBorrow off, dead connections are not detected before use. First confirm the pool still advertises them.
<|call:inspect_pool_stats|> {"service": "gateway-api", "pool": "HikariPool-1"}
System Response: active=4 idle=20 total=24, testOnBorrow=false, connectionTestQuery=null — pool reports 20 idle 'available' connections
Recovery reasoning: The pool still advertises 20 idle connections as available, but the DB restart killed their server side. Without a borrow-time test the pool hands out corpses — validate a sample to confirm.
<|call:validate_connection|> {"service": "gateway-api", "pool": "HikariPool-1", "sample": 5}
System Response: 5/5 sampled idle connections fail validation (server closed the socket); pool had not detected them
Recovery reasoning: All sampled idle connections are dead yet still 'available'. Check whether any liveness mechanism is enabled at all.
<|call:get_config|> {"service": "gateway-api", "keys": ["hikari.keepaliveTime", "hikari.maxLifetime", "hikari.connectionTestQuery"]}
System Response: keepaliveTime=0 (disabled), maxLifetime=1800000, connectionTestQuery=null — no liveness check exists
Recovery reasoning: With no keepalive and no test query, dead connections linger until first post-restart use. Enable Hikari keepalive plus a test query and soft-drain the pool so a restart is absorbed silently.
<|call:restart_pool|> {"service": "gateway-api", "action": "soft-drain", "set": {"hikari.keepaliveTime": 30000, "hikari.connectionTestQuery": "SELECT 1"}}
System Response: dead connections drained, keepalive pinging every 30s; simulated DB restart in staging yields 0 reset errors
Resolution: Root cause was the pool serving connections killed by the DB patch restart because no borrow-time test or keepalive was enabled; added 30s Hikari keepalive plus a SELECT 1 test query so the pool proactively evicts dead connections after a restart.

Reasoning: 'no more connections allowed (max_client_conn)' is a PgBouncer client-side ceiling, not a Postgres one, so Postgres being healthy is expected. The client side blew up after scaling to 80 pods. I first confirm Postgres is genuinely uninvolved.
<|call:run_sql|> {"target": "postgres", "q": "SELECT count(*) FROM pg_stat_activity"}
System Response: [OK] 142 backend connections, max_connections=500 — Postgres has tons of headroom, looks totally fine
Recovery reasoning: Postgres is fine, which is the misdirection: the limit is PgBouncer's max_client_conn for incoming app sockets, not server backends. Check PgBouncer's client-side counters.
<|call:check_pgbouncer_stats|> {"database": "checkout", "show": "clients"}
System Response: cl_active + cl_waiting = 1000/1000 (max_client_conn reached); 80 pods * 15 min-idle = 1200 desired client conns
Recovery reasoning: 80 pods each holding ~15 client connections want 1200, but max_client_conn caps inbound sockets at 1000, so new connects are refused even though Postgres is idle. Raise the cheap client-socket cap and trim per-pod min-idle.
<|call:update_config|> {"service": "pgbouncer", "key": "max_client_conn", "value": 2500, "also": {"checkout-api.hikari.minimumIdle": 2}}
System Response: max_client_conn=2500; cl_waiting=0; trimmed min-idle frees idle sockets; 0 refusals across 80 pods
Resolution: Root cause was 80 pods overrunning PgBouncer's max_client_conn=1000 client-socket ceiling while Postgres stayed idle; raised max_client_conn to 2500 and cut per-pod minimumIdle so idle sockets do not reserve client slots, keeping Postgres backends pooled at default_pool_size.
